---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
---

<!-- Linked from the footer and the AI skills card, like About. -->
<Base
  title="AI Workflow"
  description="How Sām Kazemi builds with AI: Claude Code, Codex, AI agents, and prompt engineering, with review and testing of everything they produce."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="AI in his workflow"
    title="How he builds with AI"
    lead="Sām uses AI every day, and treats it the way he treats any engineering tool: with discipline, the right configuration for the job, and a review of everything it produces."
  >
    <ul class="tag-list reveal" style="--delay: 0.15s" aria-label="AI tools">
      <li class="tag">Claude Code</li>
      <li class="tag">Codex</li>
      <li class="tag">AI agents</li>
      <li class="tag">Prompt engineering</li>
    </ul>
  </PageHeader>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">His workflow</p>
        <h2>How he works with AI</h2>
      </div>
      <figure class="ai__quote card reveal" style="--delay: 0.08s">
        <svg class="ai__mark" viewBox="0 0 48 36" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M0 36V21C0 9 6 2 18 0l2 5c-6 2-9 6-9 12h8v19H0zm28 0V21C28 9 34 2 46 0l2 5c-6 2-9 6-9 12h8v19H28z" />
        </svg>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            I use Claude Code and Codex every day as part of my engineering workflow, both to build
            new features and to maintain and audit production code. I’ve developed a structured
            approach to prompt engineering built to minimize hallucinations and AI-generated slop:
            every task gets the right context, the right constraints, and the right token budget. I
            use stronger models and deeper reasoning when I’m auditing code or reviewing a complex
            implementation, and lighter, more efficient configurations for straightforward work.
          </p>
          <p>
            I use these tools to organize repositories, write and run tests, produce implementation
            guides and sitemaps, maintain audit logs, and import or build custom skills that keep
            engineering guidelines consistent. I’ve also built AI agents that handle specific
            development tasks autonomously while operating inside clearly defined constraints.
          </p>
          <p>
            A major part of my workflow is quality control. I’m constantly looking for
            hallucinations, incorrect assumptions, duplicated or unnecessary code, and the other
            patterns that add up to slop. I review all AI-generated code and test every
            implementation myself, to catch both the obvious bugs and the “ghost bugs” that basic
            testing won’t surface. AI is an engineering tool and a collaborator, not an authority.
            The final responsibility for correctness stays with me.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption class="ai__cite">
          <span class="ai__name">Sām Kazemi</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">His take</p>
        <h2>Hiring in the age of AI</h2>
      </div>
      <!-- An argument, not a quote: one paragraph under a gold rule, no card. -->
      <p class="hiring reveal">
        In the age of AI, a lot of companies are unsure how to hire. Some still screen with
        LeetCode, no-AI policies, and no-AI coding challenges. Those can show how an engineer
        thinks about code, but real work has moved on. Very little production code is written
        fully by hand anymore, even by senior engineers, so a no-AI coding challenge is a bit like
        asking a candidate to write in binary: it tests a skill nobody will ever use on the job. It
        also misses the bigger risk. Engineers hired through no-AI screens still use AI once they
        start, like every developer does, and without understanding how models behave, the
        patterns they fall into, and how to prompt them, the result is slop: code scattered with
        ghost bugs and low-quality patterns. What a team really needs is an engineer who knows how
        to use AI the right way. I believe that is now the most valuable skill in software, even
        more than writing code by hand. I stay current on the latest models, tools, and techniques
        and keep learning new methods every day, and that is the engineer I have made myself into.
      </p>
      <p class="hiring__sign reveal">Sām Kazemi</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .tag-list {
    margin-top: 20px;
  }

  /* Hiring: one paragraph under a gold rule, set larger than the workflow card. */
  .hiring {
    margin: 0;
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--gold);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.4vw, 1.2rem);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  .hiring__sign {
    margin: 36px 0 0;
    text-align: right;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .hiring__sign::before {
    content: '— ';
    color: var(--gold-strong);
  }

  .ai__quote {
    margin: 0;
    padding: clamp(20px, 3vw, 32px);
    border-top: 4px solid var(--gold);
  }

  .ai__mark {
    width: 32px;
    height: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
    fill: var(--gold-bright);
  }

  .ai__quote blockquote {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .ai__quote blockquote p {
    margin-bottom: 0.8em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .ai__quote blockquote p:first-child {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.5vw, 1.15rem);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .ai__quote blockquote {
      column-count: 2;
      column-gap: 48px;
      column-rule: 1px solid var(--line);
    }
    .ai__quote blockquote p {
      break-inside: avoid;
    }
  }

  .ai__cite {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    margin-top: 16px;
    padding-top: 14px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .ai__name {
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
